Evaluation & acceptance criteria (suggested)

Steward acceptance rate: target > X% for auto-publish (you set X)

Quality metric: sample of N definitions manually rated for accuracy, completeness, clarity

Time saved: steward time per entry vs manual baseline

False-hallucination rate: definitions containing incorrect facts (aim for <5%)


Risks & mitigations

Hallucinations (LLM invents facts): mitigate with RAG + doc-provenance + steward approval required for low-confidence items.

Ambiguous columns: surface sample values and context; mark as “needs steward”.

Abbreviation drift / domain terms: let stewards add to abbreviation map and domain-specific glossaries.

PII / compliance: automatically flag columns via pattern rules (SSN, PAN, email regex) and require stricter stewardship flow.

Scaling & cost: run cheaper rule/RAG hybrid for high-volume, and reserve large LLM calls for ambiguous/important columns.


Practical tips & heuristics

Start with a small curated abbreviation map for your domain; it fixes many cases quickly.

Use embedding similarity between definition and docs as a sanity check.

Keep an explicit provenance field for every generated item — this builds trust with data stewards.

Prioritize tables/columns that are most used (query logs) or BI-critical to maximize business ROI.

Store steward edits as labeled training data for future fine-tuning.


